# Coding Standards

## Comments
- Minimize comments in code
- When comments are necessary, keep them concise and straightforward
- Avoid verbose or redundant comments

## Component Structure
- If a component becomes too long, split it into smaller sub-components
- Keep components focused and maintainable

## File Organization
- Separate components and their sub-components into different files
- When multiple components must be in one file:
  - Place parent components at the top
  - Place child components at the bottom

## Types and Interfaces
- Organize types and interfaces in feature-based folders/files
- Combine related types in the same file (e.g., Product with ProductOnCarts, Order with Payment)
- Don't separate types into too many files - group by feature/domain
- Keep type definitions organized and separate from component logic
- Use interfaces for object shapes, types for unions/intersections
- Prefer type inference when types are obvious
- Use `const` assertions for literal types

## Naming Conventions
- Use PascalCase for components, types, and interfaces
- Use camelCase for variables, functions, and methods
- Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constants
- Use descriptive names that indicate purpose
- Avoid abbreviations unless widely understood
- Boolean variables should start with `is`, `has`, `should`, or `can`

## React Best Practices
- Use functional components with hooks
- Extract custom hooks when logic is reusable
- Keep components pure and avoid side effects in render
- Use `useMemo` and `useCallback` judiciously (only when needed for performance)
- Only use `useEffect` when required
- Prefer composition over prop drilling
- Use proper keys in lists (stable, unique identifiers)
- Handle loading and error states explicitly
- Destructure props at the component signature

## TypeScript Best Practices
- Avoid `any` type; use `unknown` when type is truly unknown
- Use strict mode and enable all strict checks
- Prefer `interface` over `type` for object shapes (unless you need union/intersection)
- Use utility types (`Partial`, `Pick`, `Omit`, etc.) when appropriate
- Define return types for functions when not obvious
- Use `as const` for immutable data structures

## Code Quality
- Follow DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle
- Keep functions small and focused (single responsibility)
- Avoid deep nesting (max 3-4 levels)
- Use early returns to reduce nesting
- Extract magic numbers and strings into named constants
- Prefer explicit over implicit code

## Error Handling
- Handle errors gracefully with try-catch or error boundaries
- Provide meaningful error messages
- Log errors appropriately for debugging
- Use error boundaries for React component error handling
- Validate inputs at boundaries

## Performance
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders
- Use React.memo for expensive components
- Lazy load routes and heavy components
- Optimize images and assets
- Debounce/throttle expensive operations (API calls, scroll handlers)

## Security
- Sanitize user inputs
- Never expose sensitive data in client-side code
- Use environment variables for configuration
- Validate data from external sources
- Be cautious with `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`

## Accessibility
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Provide alt text for images
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Maintain proper heading hierarchy
- Use ARIA attributes when necessary
- Ensure sufficient color contrast

## File and Folder Structure
- Use feature-based or domain-based folder structure
- Group related files together
- Use index files for clean imports
- Keep file names consistent with component names
- Separate concerns (components, hooks, utils, types)

## API and Data Fetching
- Use React Query for server state management
- Handle loading, error, and success states
- Implement proper caching strategies
- Use optimistic updates when appropriate
- Validate API responses

## Testing Considerations
- Write testable code (pure functions, dependency injection)
- Keep business logic separate from UI
- Use meaningful test descriptions
- Test user behavior, not implementation details

## SonarQube Review Checklist
Before finalizing code, ensure it passes SonarQube quality gates:

### Bugs and Vulnerabilities
- Fix all critical and high-severity bugs
- Address security vulnerabilities (SQL injection, XSS, CSRF, etc.)
- Remove hardcoded credentials, tokens, or secrets
- Validate all user inputs to prevent injection attacks
- Handle null/undefined checks to prevent runtime errors

### Code Smells
- Reduce cognitive complexity (keep functions under 15 complexity)
- Eliminate duplicated code blocks
- Remove dead code and unused variables/imports
- Fix magic numbers (extract to named constants)
- Avoid deeply nested conditionals (max 3-4 levels)
- Remove commented-out code

### Maintainability
- Keep functions small (max 50 lines, ideally 20-30)
- Maintain cyclomatic complexity below 10 per function
- Ensure proper error handling in all code paths
- Use meaningful variable and function names
- Follow consistent code formatting

### Security Hotspots
- Review authentication and authorization logic
- Check for sensitive data exposure
- Validate file uploads and downloads
- Review cryptographic operations
- Audit third-party dependency vulnerabilities

### Code Coverage
- Maintain minimum code coverage threshold (typically 80%)
- Ensure critical paths are tested
- Test error handling and edge cases
- Verify integration points are covered

### TypeScript/JavaScript Specific
- Avoid `any` types (use `unknown` or proper types)
- Remove unused imports and variables
- Fix ESLint warnings and errors
- Ensure proper type guards for runtime safety
- Avoid `eval()` and `Function()` constructors

### React Specific
- Check for missing dependencies in useEffect hooks
- Ensure proper cleanup in useEffect (return cleanup functions)
- Verify keys are used correctly in lists
- Check for memory leaks (unmounted component updates)
- Review prop types and default values

### Performance
- Identify and fix performance bottlenecks
- Remove unnecessary re-renders
- Optimize expensive computations
- Review bundle size impact of new dependencies

### Final Review Steps
1. Run SonarQube analysis locally before committing
2. Address all blocker and critical issues
3. Review code smells and technical debt
4. Ensure code passes all quality gates
5. Verify no new security vulnerabilities introduced
6. Check that code coverage hasn't decreased
7. Confirm all tests pass
